      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Express $-\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Collect the common factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&-\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)\\\\&=\\sqrt{3}\\left(2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)-1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the alternative cosine double-angle formula:\n\n$$\n2\\cos^2 x-1=\\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{11\\pi}{12}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)-1&=\\cos\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)\\\\&=\\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the outside factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3}\\left(2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\right)-1\\right)&=\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
